Proposed functionality
----------------------
- Give exact native str.split, str.rsplit and str.splitlines results a finite
  homogeneous list-element contract without inventing a length or position.
- Retain source facts through captured methods, selected helper returns,
  integer indexing, slicing, ordinary loops, and a bounded eager list
  comprehension that copies or intrinsically transforms string elements.
- Keep producer authority separate from the result type. Unknown receivers,
  options, annotation-only types and unproved producers retain their boundaries.
- Revoke collection contracts through existing alias mutation and opaque escape
  handling. Scalar elements use immutable snapshots.
- Decline known empty separators and zero-step slice proofs.
- Preserve existing work limits and incomplete-analysis policy.

The patch adds 176 helper lines and removes 12; it adds 136 test lines defining
eight source-only regression methods. Existing test methods are unchanged.
No analyzed fixture program is executed by these proposed new methods.

Observed evidence and remaining limits
--------------------------------------
Before the outage, the independent CPython 3.14.4 baseline ran 28 static cases:
15 of 16 ordinary string-collection cases reported incomplete analysis; the
plain source-bearing for-loop was already complete. Three existing scalar and
literal controls completed, five unsupported controls stayed partial, and four
pathlib controls remained partial. These are BASELINE results, not repairs.

A six-file diagnostic sample on the same baseline found taint incomplete on
5/6 files, LDAP incomplete on 2/6, and SQL complete on 6/6. Repeated reasons were
opaque generator operands, delayed reads and the unchanged function work limit.
This was a bounded sample, not the strict repository self-scan.

Two source reviews found no concrete unsafe-complete path in the reviewed
candidate. That does not qualify runtime behavior. Known bounded omissions:
helper bodies which merely loop over a sequence may not request contextual value
refinement; explicit negative maxsplit syntax is outside the first argument
proof. Pathlib support and actual repository-wide improvement are deferred.
The new mutation/retained-finding test uses an earlier eval, which can itself
revoke generator authority; its partial assertion is not isolated evidence of
mutation invalidation. The stronger independent mutation cases remain frozen in
the harness below. A follow-on test-authoring task was stopped by an automatic
safety check; no result from that task is claimed.

